      "text": "To find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that fits inside a rectangle with sides lengths 10 and 11, we need to consider the maximum dimension of the rectangle. The largest possible equilateral triangle that can fit inside the rectangle will touch the rectangle along its shorter side, i.e., one of the sides of the rectangle is one of the sides of the equilateral triangle.\n\nLet's denote the side length of the equilateral triangle by \\( s \\). For the largest possible triangle, one of its vertices will touch a side of the rectangle, and the other two vertices will touch the endpoints of the opposite side. This means the height of the equilateral triangle (which is the distance from the top vertex to the bottom side of the rectangle) will be one of the sides of the rectangle.\n\nThe height \\( h \\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\( s \\) is given by the formula:\n\\[ h = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\]\n\nSince the height of the equilateral triangle must be less than or equal to the shorter side of the rectangle, we have:\n\\[ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\leq 10 \\]\n\\[ s \\leq \\frac{2 \\times 10}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{20}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\]\n\nThe area \\( A \\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\( s \\) is given by:\n\\[ A = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2 \\]\n\nSubstituting the maximum value of \\( s \\):\n\\[ A = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left( \\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{400 \\cdot 3}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1200}{9} = \\frac{300\\sqrt{3}}{9} = \\frac{100\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{100\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\]",
