      "text": "To find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that can fit inside a rectangle with sides of lengths 10 and 11, we need to consider the geometry of the problem. The maximum area will occur when the equilateral triangle shares one of its sides with the rectangle, either horizontally or vertically.\n\n1. **Case 1: The equilateral triangle shares a side with the rectangle horizontally or vertically.**\n   - If the equilateral triangle shares the bottom side of the rectangle, then the side of the triangle will be the shorter side of the rectangle, which is 10. The height \\( h \\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\( s \\) is given by \\( h = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\). For \\( s = 10 \\), the height is \\( h = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times 10 = 5\\sqrt{3} \\).\n   - The area \\( A \\) of the equilateral triangle is \\( A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 5\\sqrt{3} = 25\\sqrt{3} \\).\n\n2. **Case 2: The equilateral triangle shares a side with the rectangle at an angle.**\n   - If the equilateral triangle shares one of its sides with the rectangle, and that side is an angle side, then the base of the triangle will be the longer side of the rectangle minus twice the height of the equilateral triangle. Let's denote the side length of the equilateral triangle by \\( s \\). The height of the equilateral triangle is \\( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\), and for the base to fit inside the rectangle, the length of the rectangle minus twice the height of the triangle must be at least \\( 2s \\).\n   - The height of the triangle in this case is \\( 11 - 10 = 1 \\) (the rectangle's length minus the shorter side). So, \\( s = 2 \\times 1 = 2 \\). The area of the equilateral triangle is \\( A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 2\\sqrt{3} = 10\\sqrt{3} \\).\n\nThus, the maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle is \\( \\boxed{25\\sqrt{3}} \\). \n\nHere is the Python code to confirm this solution:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Side lengths of the rectangle\na = 10\nb = 11\n\n# Case 1: The equilateral triangle shares a side with the rectangle horizontally or vertically\n# Let s be the side length of the equilateral triangle\ns = a  # The minimum side length is the shorter side of the rectangle, which is 10\nh = (sp.sqrt(3) / 2) * s\narea1 = (1 / 2) * a * h\n\n# Case 2: The equilateral triangle shares a side with the rectangle at an angle\n# The height of the triangle in this case is 1 (the difference between the longer side of the rectangle and the shorter side)\nside_diff = b - a\ns = 2 * (side_diff / (2 * sp.sqrt(3)))  # The side length of the equilateral triangle\narea2 = (1 / 2) * a * s\n\n# The maximum area is the maximum of area1 and area2\nmax_area = sp.Max(area1, area2)\nprint(max_area)\n```",
