      "text": "To find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that fits within a rectangle \\(ABCD\\) with side lengths 10 and 11, we need to consider the possible orientations of the equilateral triangle within the rectangle.\n\nFirst, let's denote the side length of the equilateral triangle by \\(s\\). The maximum area of the equilateral triangle will occur when the triangle is oriented such that one of its sides is aligned along the length of the rectangle, either 10 or 11, and the other two sides are parallel to one of the sides of the rectangle.\n\n### Case 1: The base of the triangle is 10 and the height is \\(s\\)\nThe height \\(h\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n\\[ h = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\]\nSince the triangle is inscribed such that one of its sides is along the length of the rectangle (10), the height \\(h\\) must be less than or equal to the shorter side of the rectangle, which is 10. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\leq 10 \\]\n\\[ s \\leq \\frac{20}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\]\nThe area \\(A\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n\\[ A = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2 \\]\nSubstituting \\(s = \\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\) into the area formula, we get:\n\\[ A = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left( \\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{400 \\cdot 3}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1200}{9} = \\frac{300\\sqrt{3}}{9} = \\frac{100\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\]\n\n### Case 2: The base of the triangle is 11 and the height is \\(s\\)\nThe height \\(h\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is again given by:\n\\[ h = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\]\nSince the triangle is inscribed such that one of its sides is along the length of the rectangle (11), the height \\(h\\) must be less than or equal to the shorter side of the rectangle, which is 10. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\leq 10 \\]\n\\[ s \\leq \\frac{20}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\]\nHowever, this case is not possible because if \\(s = \\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\), then the height of the equilateral triangle would be \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{20 \\cdot 3}{6} = 10\\), which is equal to the length of the side of the rectangle, and the triangle would \"pile up\" along the length, not allowing it to fit within the width of the rectangle. Therefore, the maximum possible side length of the triangle that fits within the rectangle is \\(\\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\), and the area is:\n\\[ A = \\frac{100\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\]\n\nSince both cases yield the same maximum possible area, the maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{100\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\]",
